::: {.doc-subject}

Select the nodes or edges that match a Cypher `MATCH` pattern.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L32230-L32494){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.select_match(
    query,
    select=None,
    params=None,
    set_op="union",
    distinct_nodes=False,
    label_key="name",
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Select the matches of a pattern](../assets/graphs/select-match.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A directed six-node grid A to F with no selection, where B and C point at each other and so do D and E. After select_match with MATCH (a)-->(b)-->(a), every node in a 2-cycle is selected: B, C, D, and E, in the accent color. A and F are in no cycle and stay plain." width="100%"}

Finds every match of the pattern, as `match()` does, and selects what the matches bound:
every node of every match, or only the variables that `select=` names. The selection
then feeds the `*_ws` methods, so a query can tag, recolor or extract the parts of the
graph that fit a pattern.

The query is the same as for `match()` (see there for the supported Cypher and how it
reads the graph), except that it can't have a `RETURN` clause, since `select=` chooses
what to keep.

A graph holds a selection of nodes or a selection of edges, never both, so `select=`
names one kind:

- `select=None` (the default) selects every node in any match, including the anonymous
  ones in patterns such as `(p)-->()` and the nodes along variable-length paths. That
  highlights every occurrence of the pattern.
- `select="p"` or `select=["p", "q"]` selects only the nodes those variables matched.
- A path variable counts as a node variable, and selects every node on its paths.
- Relationship variables, as in `select="r"`, select edges instead. A variable-length
  one selects every edge on its paths.
- After `WITH`, the matches are those of the last part, and `select=` names its
  variables: those it matches and the columns of the `WITH` before it that hold nodes
  or relationships. `select=None` selects the nodes of the last part's matches, which
  include the nodes that `WITH` passes on, but not the ones that earlier parts matched
  and `WITH` dropped.

A variable that an `OPTIONAL MATCH` left `null` selects nothing.

A selection loses the pairing within each match: after selecting `a` and `b` from
`(a)-[:KNOWS]->(b)`, you can't tell who knows whom. `match()` keeps the pairs.

The selection combines with the current one according to `set_op=`, like the other
`select_*` methods. The graph is modified in place and returned for chaining.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[query]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The Cypher text, such as `"MATCH (p:Person)-[:ACTED_IN]->(m:Movie)"`. Anything outside
    the supported subset raises a `ValueError` that gives the line and column.

<code>[select]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[Union[str, list[str]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   What to select: `None` (the default) for every node of every match, or the name of a
    variable, or a list of names. The names must all be node (or path) variables or all
    be relationship variables, and can't be columns of `WITH` that hold other values.

<code>[params]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[dict]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Values for the `$name` parameters in the query, as a dict such as `{"year": 1999}`.

<code>[set_op]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;union&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   How the matches combine with the current selection. `"union"` (default) adds them to
    it, `"replace"` makes them the selection, `"intersect"` keeps only the selected
    elements among them, and `"difference"` removes them from it. Any selection of the
    other kind (edges when selecting nodes, and nodes when selecting edges) is cleared.

<code>[distinct_nodes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, the nodes of a match must all be different, as in a subgraph isomorphism.
    The default follows Cypher, where only the edges must differ.

<code>[label_key]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;name&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The property that reads the node label when a node has no attribute of that name
    (`"name"` by default, matching `from_cypher()`), or `None` to read only attributes.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The same graph (modified in place) for method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the query is invalid (as for `match()`) or has a `RETURN` clause; if `select=`
    names a variable that the query doesn't bind or that holds a value, or mixes node
    and relationship variables; or if `set_op=` is not `"union"`, `"replace"`, `"intersect"`, or
    `"difference"`. The selection is left unchanged.

<code> [TypeError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `query=` isn't a string, `select=` isn't a name or a list of names, `params=`
    isn't a dict, or `label_key=` isn't a string or `None`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~match`) 

:   The matches of a pattern, as a table.

[](`~count_matches`) 

:   Count the matches of a pattern.

[](`~select_nodes`) 

:   Select the nodes that pass a test.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

The movie graph from `match()`, with three people and two movies. Selecting the people who
acted in a movie, and tagging them:

::: {#8eb0511a .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.from_cypher("""
CREATE (keanu:Person {name: 'Keanu Reeves', born: 1964}),
       (carrie:Person {name: 'Carrie-Anne Moss', born: 1967}),
       (lana:Person {name: 'Lana Wachowski', born: 1965}),
       (matrix:Movie {title: 'The Matrix', released: 1999}),
       (wick:Movie {title: 'John Wick', released: 2014}),
       (keanu)-[:ACTED_IN {roles: ['Neo']}]->(matrix),
       (carrie)-[:ACTED_IN {roles: ['Trinity']}]->(matrix),
       (keanu)-[:ACTED_IN {roles: ['John Wick']}]->(wick),
       (lana)-[:DIRECTED]->(matrix)
""")

(
    graph
    .select_match("MATCH (p:Person)-[:ACTED_IN]->(:Movie)", select="p")
    .set_node_attrs_ws(actor=True)
    .get_node_df()
    .select("label", "actor")
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```{=html}
